That is, we want:

$$
\gcd(4n+5, 7n+6) > 1
$$

Let us define:

$$
d = \gcd(4n+5, 7n+6)
$$

Then $ d \mid (4n+5) $ and $ d \mid (7n+6) $. So $ d $ divides any integer linear combination of these two.

Let's eliminate $ n $. Use the Euclidean algorithm:

$$
d \mid (7n+6) - (4n+5) \cdot 1 = 3n + 1
$$
So $ d \mid 3n + 1 $

Also, $ d \mid 4n + 5 $

